org.openelisglobal.fhir.subscriber=${BRIDGE_FHIR_BASE}
# Minutes between scheduled retries for failed FHIR exports. Lower values
# give faster recovery from outages but increase load on the partner FHIR
# server. 5 matches the historical default in RegisterFhirHooksTask; tune
# downward only after measuring partner capacity.
org.openelisglobal.fhir.subscriber.backup.interval=${OE_SUBSCRIBER_BACKUP_INTERVAL}
# Organization is here for REFERRAL (send-out) testing, and it has to be
# subscribed BEFORE the first send-out rather than after.
#
# When our laboratory sends a specimen to another laboratory, OpenELIS records
# the receiving lab as an `organization` tagged organization_type=referralLab,
# and publishes a Task for the referral (FhirReferralServiceImpl). Task is
# already on this list, so the referral signal itself would arrive. The
# Organization naming the laboratory would not.
#
# Why now, with no send-outs yet: resources are pushed when they CHANGE. A
# reference laboratory configured last month is not re-pushed merely because we
# started listening today, so subscribing after the first referral can leave us
# holding a Task that points at an Organization we never received.
#
# What this does NOT solve: DiagnosticReport.performer and Observation.performer
# are never set anywhere in OpenELIS - no FHIR field on a result says who
# actually performed it. Naming the performing laboratory means correlating the
# result back through the referral Task, and ISO 15189 7.4.1.7.c and CLIA
# 42 CFR 493.1291(i)(3) both require that name on the report. Filed upstream.
org.openelisglobal.fhir.subscriber.resources=Task,Patient,ServiceRequest,DiagnosticReport,Observation,Specimen,Practitioner,Encounter,Organization

